    "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nPass vLLM's configured media byte limits to the supervised Rust frontend. The pinned multimodal crate already rejects oversized responses while streaming, but its default audio ceiling is 256 MiB rather than vLLM's 25 MiB default.\n\n## Claims\n\n- The Rust child receives the smaller of the vLLM media download limit and the audio filesize limit for audio, and the media download limit for image and video.\n- Existing stricter crate limits remain in force; values above the crate's 256 MiB default do not loosen its protection.\n- The process environment of the Python supervisor is unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=cpu .../.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/entrypoints/launchers/api_server/test_api_server_process_manager.py -q --basetemp=/tmp/vllm-rust-media-tests-full`: 16 passed on macOS.\n- `pre-commit run --files vllm/v1/utils.py tests/entrypoints/launchers/api_server/test_api_server_process_manager.py`: passed.\n- `pre-commit run --all-files`: unrelated macOS failures in mypy (`MADV_HUGEPAGE`, `posix_fadvise`) and the nonroot entrypoint test (`/var` versus `/private/var`).\n- Full Rust frontend serving reproduction remains to be run on a supported deployment before marking this draft ready for review.\n- No model evaluation was run; the change affects media admission in the Rust frontend, and no generated output was evaluated.\n\n## Details\n\nThe Rust frontend constructs the pinned multimodal connector using its defaults. Its audio input cap is 256 MiB, while vLLM documents a 25 MiB default compressed audio cap. The launcher now sets the crate's input-limit environment variables before starting the Rust child, so all HTTP and gRPC routes that share that connector receive the same bound. The change also honors a lower operator-configured crate cap.\n\nThe crate's input cap applies to local and inline media as well as remote media. Thus an operator who sets a lower general download limit will also lower those inputs in the Rust frontend. The 256 MiB crate default remains the ceiling when vLLM is configured higher.\n\nThe change does not add an aggregate request or process budget. It closes the discrepancy between the documented per-item limits and the Rust connector's effective limits; aggregate media resource use is a separate concern.\n\nDuplicate-work check: reviewed the diffs for PRs #51896, #49855, #59854, #46836, and #51894. None forwards these byte limits into the Rust child.\n\nThis PR was developed with AI assistance.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill.\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Reuses the pinned crate's existing streaming size check.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** Full Rust serving reproduction remains pending.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Targeted hooks pass; no new public API.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes root cause, coverage, duplicate check, and limitations.\n</details>\n\n",
